    "case_id": "325",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 70s presented with an enlarged and variegated plaque on her left axilla, which she had had for 4 years. The lesion started as a small eczematous, pink-to-brown plaque. As the lesion was asymptomatic and slow growing, she did not initially seek treatment. Recently, the color of the lesion had changed to an alarming dark brown color. No breast mass or lymphadenopathy was observed. Her medical and family history was unremarkable.\n\nEnlarged and variegated plaque present on left axilla with well-demarcated margins, dark brown in color. No breast mass or lymphadenopathy detected.\n\n- Histopathology:    - Incisional biopsy revealed an intraepidermal neoplasm consisting of pagetoid cells, characterized by round, pale vacuolated cytoplasm and large pleomorphic nuclei      - Immunohistochemical staining:    - Positive for CK7, EMA    - Surrounded by numerous reactive dendritic HMB-45–positive melanocytes scattered among tumor cells      - Images:    Image A Title: Clinical presentation of pigmented plaque    Image A Description: Pigmented plaque in the axilla with well-demarcated margins        Image B Title: Histologic findings    Image B Description: Intraepidermal neoplasm with pagetoid cells (hematoxylin-eosin; original magnification ×200)        Image C Title: Immunohistochemical findings    Image C Description: Positive staining for EMA (original magnification ×200)        Image D Title: HMB-45 staining    Image D Description: Numerous reactive dendritic HMB-45–positive melanocytes scattered among tumor cells (original magnification ×200)",
